1
00:00:02,060 --> 00:00:02,820
Reading.

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:06,840
Communication, no problem.

3
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:14,520
Yesterday, another student and I, representing our university's student association, went

4
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:19,160
to the Capital International Airport to meet this year's international students.

5
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:23,180
They were coming to study at Beijing University.

6
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:28,320
We would take them first to their dormitories and then to the student canteen.

7
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:32,520
After half an hour of waiting for their flight to arrive,

8
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:38,420
I saw several young people enter the waiting area looking around curiously.

9
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:42,920
I stood for a minute watching them and then went to greet them.

10
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:54,400
After I met them and then introduced them to each other,

11
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:56,120
I was very surprised.

12
00:00:56,920 --> 00:01:01,460
Tony approached Julia, touched her shoulder and kissed her on the cheek!

13
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:17,820
Then Akira Nagata from Japan came in smiling,

14
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:20,080
together with George Cook from Canada.

15
00:01:20,730 --> 00:01:25,440
As they were introduced, George reached his hand out to the Japanese student.

16
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:27,680
Just at that moment, however,

17
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:31,680
Akira bowed so his nose touched George’s moving hand.

18
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:35,840
They both apologized - another cultural mistake!

19
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:41,360
Ahmed Aziz, another international student,

20
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:42,560
was from Jordan.

21
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:47,520
When we met yesterday, he moved very close to me as I introduced myself.

22
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:53,420
I moved back a bit, but he came closer to ask a question and then shook my hand.

23
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:57,940
When Darlene Coulon from France came dashing through the door,

24
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:01,000
she recognized Tony Garcia’s smiling face.

25
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:05,200
They shook hands and then kissed each other twice on each cheek,

26
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:09,140
since that is the French custom when adults meet people they know.

27
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:13,480
Ahmed Aziz, on the contrary, simply nodded at the girls.

28
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:20,140
Men from Middle Eastern and other Muslim countries will often stand quite close to other men to talk

29
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:22,440
but will usually not touch women.

30
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:26,840
As I get to know more international friends,

31
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:29,820
I learn more about this cultural "body language".

32
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:34,000
Not all cultures greet each other the same way,

33
00:02:34,040 --> 00:02:38,540
nor are they comfortable in the same way with touching or distance between people.

34
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:42,520
In the same way that people communicate with spoken language,

35
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:47,580
they also express their feelings using unspoken "language" through physical distance,

36
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:49,120
actions or posture.

37
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:51,680
English people, for example,

38
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:57,380
do not usually stand very close to others or touch strangers as soon as they meet.

39
00:02:57,580 --> 00:02:59,980
However, people from places like Spain,

40
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:06,140
Italy or South American countries approach others closely and are more likely to touch them.

41
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:11,060
Most people around the world now greet each other by shaking hands,

42
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:14,180
but some cultures use other greetings as well,

43
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:16,840
such as the Japanese, who prefer to bow.

44
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,100
These actions are not good or bad,

45
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:22,920
but are simply ways in which cultures have developed.

46
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:27,800
I have seen, however, that cultural customs for body language are very general.

47
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:31,180
Not all members of a culture behave in the same way.

48
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:36,940
In general, though, studying international customs can certainly help avoid difficulties

49
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:39,860
in today's world of cultural crossroads.
